<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="8-O-methylated benzoxazinoid glucoside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Benzoxazinoids were identified in the early 1960s as secondary metabolites of the grasses (including the major agricultural crops maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565|), and rye (|FRAME: TAX-4550|)) that function as natural pesticides and exhibit allelopathic properties |CITS:[BARNES87][10804466]|. Benzoxazinoids are synthesized in two subfamilies of the |FRAME: TAX-4479| and sporadically in single species of the dicots |CITS: [19577780]|. They play an important role in defense against pests such as insects, pathogenic fungi and bacteria |CITS:[niemeyer88]|. The most common benzoxazinoids are |FRAME: CPD-6365| and |FRAME: CPD-6366|. Since the aglycone benzoxazinoids (see |FRAME:PWY-4161|) are autotoxic, they are stored in glucosylated forms that have reduced chemical reactivity |CITS: [10804466]|. The glucosides are stored in the vacuoles |CITS:[12239364]|. This allows the plants to store the toxic compounds in anticipation of pathogen attacks, prompting the suggestion to name these compounds &quot;phytoanticipins&quot;. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-22324| and |FRAME: CPD-22325| are two 8-O-methylated benzoxazinoid glucosides produced in maize. They are derived from another benzoxazinoid glucoside, |FRAME: CPD-6562|, via the intermediate |FRAME: CPD-22323|. Benzoxazinoids serve as anti-herbivore defense compounds in grasses, and the 8-O-methylated compounds produced by this pathway appear to target phloem-feeding insects specifically, as they were found to affect the reproductive rate of aphids but not the chewing insect pests such as the armyworm |CIT: 27317675|.</Description>
